      "title": "Roman Republic",
      "periodLabel": "200–100 BCE observations",
      "summary": "Rome governed a growing Mediterranean domain through republican institutions. Its ruling elite included patrician and wealthy plebeian families; territorial expansion brought resources and mounting political pressures.",
      "institutions": [
        "Magistracies, the Senate and citizen assemblies formed the republican system.",
        "Territories outside Italy were administered as provinces as conquest and diplomacy extended Roman authority."
      ],
      "changes": [
        "The political accommodation of patrician and wealthy plebeian families preceded these observations; later civil wars would transform the republic."
      ],
